<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yung Wing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rong Hong</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yung Wing was a Chinese-American diplomat and businessman who in 1854 became the first recorded Chinese graduate of an American university, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale College</a>. In 1863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a> sent him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to buy machinery, which went into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal">Jiangnan Arsenal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. He persuaded the Qing government to send young students abroad and organised the Chinese Educational Mission, which brought 120 of them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> from 1872 until it was disbanded in 1881. A naturalised American citizen from 1852, he lost that status under the Chinese Exclusion Act.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yung-wing.jpg" alt="Yung Wing" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fred Hsu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yung_Wing_Frontispiece_My_Life_in_China_and_America_1909_FRD_4814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yung Wing, whose name is Rong Hong in pinyin, was born on 17 November 1828 and had his early schooling at a mission school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a>. His studies at Yale were sponsored by Samuel Robbins Brown, and he was a member and librarian of Brothers in Unity, a Yale literary society. After graduating he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> and worked as an interpreter with Western missionaries; the linguist Stephen Pearl Andrews suspected he was the first Chinese person to reach near mastery of English.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1859 Yung went to the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> rebels at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, but all his proposals for making the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom more efficient were refused. The arsenal he equipped with machinery bought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a> later became the Jiangnan Shipyard. In 1874 he and the Hartford pastor Joseph Twichell travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> to look into the conditions of Chinese coolies there, which were brutal and led to strikes and violent suppression. Many students of his Chinese Educational Mission returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and contributed to the civil service, engineering and science.</p><p>A lifelong supporter of reform, Yung followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>, whom he called the great pioneer of reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>'s coup of 1898 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a>, a price of $70,000 was put on his head and he fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> for British Hong Kong. In 1908 he joined Homer Lea, a former military adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a>, in the Red Dragon Plan, a conspiracy to raise an army and conquer Liangguang with Yung at the head of a coalition government; it collapsed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1876 Yung married Mary Kellogg, with whom he had two sons, Morrison Brown Yung and Bartlett Golden Yung, and that year Yale gave him an honorary Doctor of Laws. A 1902 letter told him his citizenship had been revoked, but with friends' help he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in time to see Bartlett graduate from Yale. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang-uprising">Wuchang Uprising</a> in 1911 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> invited him to join the new government; he declined, citing age and poor health, and sent his two sons instead. He died in poverty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hartford-connecticut">Hartford, Connecticut</a>, on 21 April 1912 and is buried in Cedar Hill Cemetery. His memoir, My Life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and America, appeared in 1909.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yung Wing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Anqing in 1861; in 1865 he and Li Hongzhang founded the Jiangnan Arsenal at Shanghai, with machinery bought in the United States by <b>Yung Wing</b>, and Li founded another arsenal at Nanjing; in 1866 Zuo Zongtang founded the Fuzhou Navy Yard, with French help and a naval school.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zhuhai, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hartford-connecticut">Hartford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.27,113.57&amp;year=1882" aria-label="Zhuhai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Yung Wing"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4492 5644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4492 5644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.27,113.57&amp;year=1882">What was here in 1882? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I was determined that the rising generation of China should enjoy the same educational advantages that I had enjoyed; that through western education China might be regenerated, become enlightened and powerful.&#8221;</p><footer>My Life in China and America (1909), ch. 5 &#183; original &#183; the first Chinese graduate of an American college, Yale 1854 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/54635/pg54635-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The more one knows, the more he suffers and is consequently less happy; the less one knows, the less he suffers, and hence is more happy.&#8221;</p><footer>My Life in China and America (1909), ch. 5 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/54635/pg54635-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…neither my patriotism nor the love of my fellow-countrymen had been weakened. On the contrary, they had increased in strength from sympathy.&#8221;</p><footer>My Life in China and America (1909), preface &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/54635/pg54635-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
